  pmid         = {16030331},
  issn         = {0090-0036,1541-0048},
  abstract     = {Concepts of cause and causal inference are largely self-taught
                  from early learning experiences. A model of causation that
                  describes causes in terms of sufficient causes and their
                  component causes illuminates important principles such as
                  multi-causality, the dependence of the strength of component
                  causes on the prevalence of complementary component causes,
                  and interaction between component causes. Philosophers agree
                  that causal propositions cannot be proved, and find flaws or
                  practical limitations in all philosophies of causal inference.
